{"version":1,"reference":{"kind":"artifact_content","namespace":"#1632","artifact_type":"_dh-work-item-head-v1","name":"head"},"owner_reference":"#1632","content":"{\"version\":1,\"issue_reference\":\"#1632\",\"parent_revision\":\"7c8618bc8596545e2fd6b363089f490410094dd379aa2d667a495f2cc26f184e\",\"root_revision\":\"7c8618bc8596545e2fd6b363089f490410094dd379aa2d667a495f2cc26f184e\",\"body\":\"<!-- backlog-metadata:\\npriority: \\ntype: Feature\\nstatus: closed\\nadded: \\n-->\\n\\n## Description\\n\\nThe three backlog lifecycle skills (create-backlog-item, groom-backlog-item, work-backlog-item) have divergent procedures, inconsistent references, and duplicated content. Consolidate under a single entry point (work-backlog-item) with self-contained workflow files per stage (create, groom, work). Each workflow stage gets its own directory with scope boundary, start checklist, and procedure files. The old skills become thin redirects.\\n\\n## Fact-Check\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\nverdict: VERIFIED\\nclaim: \\\"create-backlog-item, groom-backlog-item are currently thin redirects to work-backlog-item\\\"\\nevidence: create-backlog-item/SKILL.md is 9 lines; body is `Invoke /dh:work-backlog-item create $ARGUMENTS`. groom-backlog-item/SKILL.md is 9 lines; body is `Invoke /dh:work-backlog-item groom $ARGUMENTS`.\\nsource: plugins/development-harness/skills/create-backlog-item/SKILL.md:1-9, plugins/development-harness/skills/groom-backlog-item/SKILL.md:1-9\\n\\n---\\n\\nverdict: VERIFIED\\nclaim: \\\"The three backlog lifecycle skills have divergent procedures\\\"\\nevidence: create/start.md contains only inline steps (no Mermaid flowchart, no file delegation table). groom/start.md contains a Mermaid flowchart, a process document table, and a numbered checklist. work/start.md contains a checklist that delegates to named files but no flowchart and no process table. All three have structurally distinct formats.\\nsource: references/workflows/create/start.md, references/workflows/groom/start.md:1-100, references/workflows/work/start.md:1-60\\n\\n---\\n\\nverdict: VERIFIED\\nclaim: \\\"inconsistent references\\\"\\nevidence: create/start.md references no other files. groom/start.md references 8 files (scope.md, intake.md, analyze.md, swarm.md, finalize.md, error.md, finally.md, groom-drift.md). work/start.md references 4 files (locate.md, validate.md, prepare.md, plan.md). The create stage is entirely self-contained while groom and work use file-based delegation.\\nsource: references/workflows/create/start.md, references/workflows/groom/start.md:29-40, references/workflows/work/start.md:7-30\\n\\n---\\n\\nverdict: VERIFIED\\nclaim: \\\"duplicated content\\\"\\nevidence: create/start.md embeds a full scope boundary section inline (lines 5-45, titled \\\"Create - backlog item - Scope boundary\\\"). groom/ has an equivalent scope boundary in a separate file (scope.md). The scope boundary concept appears in both create and groom but handled differently \u2014 inline in create, as a separate file in groom.\\nsource: references/workflows/create/start.md:5-45, references/workflows/groom/scope.md:1-35\\n\\n---\\n\\nverdict: VERIFIED\\nclaim: \\\"create/ has only start.md combining scope boundary + all procedure steps inline\\\"\\nevidence: Glob of references/workflows/create/ returned exactly 1 file: start.md. The file contains scope boundary inline (lines 5-45) followed by Steps 1-6 with all procedure detail inline. No separate scope.md or sub-procedure files.\\nsource: references/workflows/create/start.md (sole file in create/)\\n\\n---\\n\\nverdict: VERIFIED\\nclaim: \\\"groom/ has scope.md + start.md + procedure files (the reference pattern)\\\"\\nevidence: Glob of references/workflows/groom/ returned 9 files: analyze.md, error.md, start.md, scope.md, finalize.md, swarm.md, intake.md, groom-drift.md, finally.md. scope.md and start.md both present; multiple procedure files present.\\nsource: plugins/development-harness/skills/work-backlog-item/references/workflows/groom/ (9 files)\\n\\n---\\n\\nverdict: REFUTED (partial \u2014 no scope.md verified, file count wrong)\\nclaim: \\\"work/ has start.md + 15 procedure files but no scope.md\\\"\\nevidence: Glob of references/workflows/work/ returned 22 files total (start.md + 21 other files). No scope.md is present \u2014 that part is correct. But the count \\\"15 procedure files\\\" is wrong: there are 21 files other than start.md (already-implemented.md, groom-check.md, post-planning.md, find-item.md, auto-mode.md, commit-conventions.md, error-handling.md, locate.md, examples.md, feasibility-gate.md, prepare.md, rt-ica-gate.md, sam-definition.md, feature-request.md, field-mapping.md, validate.md, interactive-browser.md, validation.md, github-sync.md, issue-first.md, plan.md).\\nsource: plugins/development-harness/skills/work-backlog-item/references/workflows/work/ (22 files enumerated)\\n</div>\\n\\n## RT-ICA\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\nRT-ICA Assessment: refactor: Improve the backlog-item intake process to consolidate workflows and align all steps\\nDate: 2026-04-06\\nGoal: Extract scope.md from create/start.md and add scope.md to work/, aligning all three workflow stages with the groom/ reference pattern (scope.md + start.md checklist + procedure files).\\nConditions:\\n1. Scope boundary content location in create/start.md (lines 5\u201345) | Status: AVAILABLE\\n2. Structural reference pattern (groom/scope.md + groom/start.md + groom Mermaid first node) | Status: AVAILABLE\\n3. work/start.md current content and checklist insertion point for Step 0 \\\"Read scope.md\\\" | Status: AVAILABLE\\n4. Content for work/scope.md (derivable from groom/scope.md as template + work/ procedure file inventory of 21 files) | Status: DERIVABLE\\n5. Cross-file reference consistency \u2014 no broken links introduced by the refactor (verified: paths unchanged, only internal structure reorganizes) | Status: DERIVABLE\\n6. Whether create/start.md procedure steps need decomposition: work/ already has 21 procedure files; scope extraction only is sufficient for this item | Status: DERIVABLE\\n7. create/start.md inline self-check guard text \u2014 exact guard text and update strategy after extraction | Status: DERIVABLE\\n8. work-backlog-item/SKILL.md Mermaid create-stage diagram \u2014 must gain Scope[\\\"Read scope.md\\\"] first node to match groom diagram | Status: DERIVABLE\\n9. docs/backlog-lifecycle.md Stage Definitions table create row \u2014 must be updated to mention scope.md | Status: DERIVABLE\\n10. groom/error.md scope-boundary-presence guard \u2014 must be read to confirm trigger condition before editing | Status: DERIVABLE\\nAVAILABLE count: 3\\nDERIVABLE count: 7\\nMISSING count: 0\\nDecision: APPROVED\\n</div>\\n\\n## Impact Radius\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n### Code \u2014 Producers\\n\\n- `plugins/development-harness/skills/work-backlog-item/references/workflows/create/start.md` \u2014 currently contains the \\\"Create - backlog item - Scope boundary\\\" section inline; this file is the sole producer of create-stage scope rules. Extracting scope content to a new `create/scope.md` changes its internal structure and line count. | Risk: MEDIUM | Why: The scope boundary section is referenced by path from `error.md`'s self-check (`scope boundary rules section missing from this workflow file`) \u2014 removal changes what that guard detects.\\n\\n- `plugins/development-harness/skills/work-backlog-item/references/workflows/groom/scope.md` \u2014 reference pattern for the new `create/scope.md` and `work/scope.md` files; its structure is the specification that the new files must match. | Risk: LOW | Why: Read-only reference; no changes to this file needed.\\n\\n- `plugins/development-harness/skills/work-backlog-item/references/workflows/work/start.md` \u2014 currently a checklist-only file with no scope section; adding a `scope.md` parallel requires adding a Step 0 \\\"Read scope.md\\\" to its checklist to align with the groom pattern. | Risk: MEDIUM | Why: Every step in the checklist is directly executed by AI agents \u2014 omitting the Read step means the scope boundary is never loaded.\\n\\n- `plugins/development-harness/skills/work-backlog-item/references/workflows/groom/start.md` \u2014 already has the `scope.md` pattern in its checklist (Step 1: \\\"Read scope.md\\\") and Mermaid flow. No structural change required; it is the reference pattern. | Risk: LOW | Why: Existing correct state; verify it stays consistent after create/work are aligned.\\n\\n### Code \u2014 Consumers\\n\\n- `plugins/development-harness/skills/work-backlog-item/SKILL.md` \u2014 routes execution to `references/workflows/create/start.md`, `references/workflows/groom/start.md`, and `references/workflows/work/start.md` by file path via Mermaid flowchart nodes. If `create/start.md` is restructured (scope extracted), the routing target path does not change but downstream behavior changes. | Risk: LOW | Why: SKILL.md references the file by path only; it does not depend on internal structure.\\n\\n- `plugins/development-harness/skills/work-backlog-item/scripts/parser/command-routes.json` \u2014 maps route keys (`create`, `groom`, `work`) to their `start.md` paths. No path changes in this refactor. | Risk: LOW | Why: Paths are unchanged; only file contents reorganize.\\n\\n- `plugins/development-harness/skills/work-backlog-item/references/workflows/work/prepare.md` \u2014 invokes `references/workflows/groom/start.md` by path for auto-groom. No changes to groom's entry path. | Risk: LOW | Why: Invocation path unchanged.\\n\\n- `plugins/development-harness/skills/work-backlog-item/references/workflows/groom/error.md` \u2014 contains a self-check: reports \\\"scope boundary rules section missing from this workflow file.\\\" After extraction, this guard in `create/start.md` must either remain or be updated to reference `scope.md` instead. If the guard is left in `create/start.md` as a cross-reference to `scope.md`, no breakage. If the section is fully removed from `create/start.md`, the guard fires incorrectly. | Risk: HIGH | Why: The guard is a runtime validation step executed by AI agents; if it fires after extraction, it blocks the create workflow with a false alarm.\\n\\n### Code \u2014 Other References\\n\\n- `plugins/development-harness/skills/work-backlog-item/references/workflows/groom/intake.md` \u2014 validates the item and checks groom eligibility. Does not reference create/start.md. | Risk: LOW | Why: No dependency on create workflow internal structure.\\n\\n- `plugins/development-harness/skills/work-backlog-item/references/workflows/groom/finalize.md` \u2014 post-swarm gate and write step; reads the groom workflow checklist indirectly. No direct reference to create/start.md scope content. | Risk: LOW | Why: Operates within the groom workflow boundary only.\\n\\n### Documentation (will become stale)\\n\\n- `plugins/development-harness/docs/backlog-lifecycle.md` \u2014 references `skills/work-backlog-item/references/workflows/create/start.md` in its frontmatter `related:` section and in the Stage Definitions table. The reference is to the file path (unchanged), but the document also describes the create stage structure implicitly. If a `create/scope.md` is added, the Stage Definitions table row for Create should mention it as it does for Groom. | Risk: MEDIUM | Why: The table at line 143 documents the create stage workflow file but makes no mention of scope.md \u2014 it will be inconsistent with the groom row after alignment.\\n\\n- `plugins/development-harness/docs/backlog-item-lifecycle.md` \u2014 sources `create-backlog-item/SKILL.md` and `work-backlog-item/SKILL.md`. Mentions create-stage skill entry points. Will not break but may become misleadingly incomplete if the scope.md alignment is not reflected. | Risk: LOW | Why: High-level lifecycle doc; not a procedural reference.\\n\\n- `plugins/development-harness/docs/process-audit-backlog-lifecycle-2026-03-02.md` \u2014 references \\\"scope boundary\\\" as a quality check dimension. No path references. | Risk: LOW | Why: Audit/historical doc; not consumed by agents at runtime.\\n\\n### Configuration / CI\\n\\n- `.github/workflows/` (all 7 workflows) \u2014 none reference workflow file paths directly. No CI workflow reads or validates `create/start.md`, `work/start.md`, or `groom/scope.md` by path. | Risk: LOW | Why: CI validates Python code and plugin linting, not skill markdown file structure.\\n\\n### Agent Instructions\\n\\n- `plugins/development-harness/agents/impact-analyst.md` \u2014 spawned by the groom workflow; does not reference create/start.md or work/start.md internals. No update needed. | Risk: LOW | Why: The agent reads backlog item content via MCP, not workflow files.\\n\\n- `plugins/development-harness/skills/create-backlog-item/SKILL.md` \u2014 thin redirector: `Invoke /dh:work-backlog-item create $ARGUMENTS`. Does not reference internal workflow structure. No update needed. | Risk: LOW | Why: Single-line delegation; no structural dependency.\\n\\n- `plugins/development-harness/skills/groom-backlog-item/SKILL.md` \u2014 thin redirector: `Invoke /dh:work-backlog-item groom $ARGUMENTS`. No update needed. | Risk: LOW | Why: Single-line delegation.\\n\\n- `plugins/development-harness/skills/work-backlog-item/SKILL.md` \u2014 the main AI-facing instruction file. Its Mermaid routing diagram names workflow files by path. The `create` route diagram refers to `references/workflows/create/start.md` with no mention of a scope.md step. After the create stage gains a `scope.md`, this diagram should reflect a \\\"Read scope.md\\\" step to stay consistent with the groom diagram which already shows `Scope[\\\"Read scope.md\\\"]` as the first node. | Risk: MEDIUM | Why: Agents treat Mermaid diagrams as authoritative executable procedures; the diagram omitting a scope.md step means agents won't load it.\\n\\n- `plugins/development-harness/skills/development-harness/SKILL.md` \u2014 skill router; describes the create stage at a high level. No procedural reference to internal workflow file structure. | Risk: LOW | Why: Describes intent routing, not step-level procedure.\\n\\n- `plugins/development-harness/skills/groom-backlog-item/references/groomer-output-validation.md` \u2014 validates section presence post-groom; not affected by create/work scope extraction. | Risk: LOW | Why: Operates only on groom output sections.\\n\\n### Systems Inventory\\n\\n- `plugins/development-harness/skills/work-backlog-item/references/workflows/create/start.md` | Role: producer | Connection: File being structurally modified \u2014 scope section extracted to new `create/scope.md` | Action: CODE_CHANGE | Risk: MEDIUM\\n- `plugins/development-harness/skills/work-backlog-item/references/workflows/work/start.md` | Role: producer | Connection: File being aligned \u2014 `scope.md` step added to checklist | Action: CODE_CHANGE | Risk: MEDIUM\\n- `plugins/development-harness/skills/work-backlog-item/references/workflows/groom/start.md` | Role: producer (reference pattern) | Connection: Authoritative pattern for scope.md integration in workflow start files | Action: VERIFY_COMPATIBLE | Risk: LOW\\n- `plugins/development-harness/skills/work-backlog-item/references/workflows/groom/scope.md` | Role: producer (reference pattern) | Connection: Structural template for new create/scope.md and work/scope.md | Action: VERIFY_COMPATIBLE | Risk: LOW\\n- `plugins/development-harness/skills/work-backlog-item/references/workflows/groom/error.md` | Role: consumer | Connection: Contains scope boundary self-check guard that must be preserved or updated after extraction | Action: CONTENT_UPDATE | Risk: HIGH\\n- `plugins/development-harness/skills/work-backlog-item/SKILL.md` | Role: consumer | Connection: Mermaid routing diagram for create/groom/work pipelines; create diagram lacks scope.md step | Action: CONTENT_UPDATE | Risk: MEDIUM\\n- `plugins/development-harness/skills/work-backlog-item/scripts/parser/command-routes.json` | Role: configuration | Connection: Maps route keys to start.md paths; paths unchanged | Action: VERIFY_COMPATIBLE | Risk: LOW\\n- `plugins/development-harness/docs/backlog-lifecycle.md` | Role: documentation | Connection: Stage Definitions table row for create stage will be inconsistent after groom/create/work alignment | Action: CONTENT_UPDATE | Risk: MEDIUM\\n- `plugins/development-harness/agents/impact-analyst.md` | Role: agent-instruction | Connection: Spawned by groom workflow; no dependency on create/work internal structure | Action: VERIFY_COMPATIBLE | Risk: LOW\\n- `plugins/development-harness/skills/create-backlog-item/SKILL.md` | Role: agent-instruction | Connection: Thin redirector to work-backlog-item create route; no structural dependency | Action: VERIFY_COMPATIBLE | Risk: LOW\\n- `plugins/development-harness/skills/groom-backlog-item/SKILL.md` | Role: agent-instruction | Connection: Thin redirector to work-backlog-item groom route; no structural dependency | Action: VERIFY_COMPATIBLE | Risk: LOW\\n- `plugins/development-harness/skills/development-harness/SKILL.md` | Role: agent-instruction | Connection: Skill router; high-level description only | Action: VERIFY_COMPATIBLE | Risk: LOW\\n- `plugins/development-harness/tests/test_scenarios.py` | Role: test | Connection: Groups 2.1 (create), 2.2\u20132.5 (work), 2.6 (groom) test MCP tool behavior \u2014 no tests exercise workflow file loading or scope boundary enforcement | Action: VERIFY_COMPATIBLE | Risk: LOW\\n\\n### Risk Summary\\n\\n- Overall system risk: MEDIUM\\n- Highest-risk systems:\\n  - `plugins/development-harness/skills/work-backlog-item/references/workflows/groom/error.md` \u2014 contains a runtime self-check guard (`scope boundary rules section missing from this workflow file`) that will fire as a false alarm if the scope section is removed from `create/start.md` without updating the guard\\n  - `plugins/development-harness/skills/work-backlog-item/SKILL.md` \u2014 Mermaid routing diagram is an executable procedure; the create stage diagram must gain a `scope.md` read step to match the groom pattern or agents will skip loading the scope boundary\\n- Main risk themes:\\n  - behavioral (agent skips scope.md if not in diagram)\\n  - documentation (backlog-lifecycle.md table inconsistency)\\n  - verification (no tests cover workflow file loading or scope boundary enforcement at the AI agent level)\\n- Scope expansion:\\n  - The `error.md` self-check guard is a dependency not mentioned in the item description \u2014 it is a runtime validation step that will break if the scope section location changes without a corresponding update to the guard text\\n\\n### Ecosystem Completeness Checklist\\n\\n- [ ] Every code producer updated or verified compatible\\n- [ ] Every code consumer migrated or verified compatible\\n- [ ] Every affected test updated or new coverage added\\n- [ ] Every stale document updated\\n- [ ] Every affected config or CI path updated and validated\\n- [ ] Every affected agent or skill instruction updated\\n- [ ] Replacement or migration coverage gaps resolved\\n- [ ] Hidden transitive dependencies checked\\n</div>\\n\\n## Acceptance Criteria\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n- [ ] Work matches description\\n- [ ] Plan or implementation complete\\n</div>\\n\\n## Story\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\nAs a **maintainer of the codebase**, I want to **improve the backlog-item intake process to consolidate workflows and align all steps** so that **the code is cleaner and more maintainable**.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Context\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n- **Source**: Session 2026-04-06 \u2014 inconsistency analysis across 3 skills and their reference files\\n- **Priority**: P1\\n- **Added**: 2026-04-06\\n- **Research questions**: None\\n\\n<!-- artifact-gist:58130c6e111034dae93369c740e0012f -->\\n</div>\\n\\n## Groomed (2026-04-07)\\n\\n### Reproducibility\\n\\n1. Navigate to `plugins/development-harness/skills/work-backlog-item/references/workflows/`.\\n2. List files in `create/` \u2014 observe only `start.md` exists (no `scope.md`).\\n3. List files in `work/` \u2014 observe `start.md` plus 21 procedure files, but no `scope.md`.\\n4. List files in `groom/` \u2014 observe `scope.md` + `start.md` + 7 procedure files (the reference pattern).\\n5. Open `create/start.md` \u2014 observe the \\\"Create - backlog item - Scope boundary\\\" section embedded inline (lines 5\u201345) alongside all six procedure steps.\\n6. Open `groom/start.md` \u2014 observe Step 1 is \\\"Read scope.md\\\"; scope rules live in a separate file.\\n7. Open `work/start.md` \u2014 observe no \\\"Read scope.md\\\" step; scope rules are absent entirely.\\n8. Open `work-backlog-item/SKILL.md` \u2014 observe the create-stage Mermaid diagram has no `Scope[\\\"Read scope.md\\\"]` first node, while the groom-stage diagram does.\\n\\n### Priority\\n\\n**Priority**: 7/10\\n**Effort**: Small\\n\\nThe three workflow stages are actively executed by AI agents on every backlog operation. The structural inconsistency means scope boundaries are absent from the work stage and inline-only in the create stage, creating behavioral gaps every time an agent runs those paths. The groom stage is the only stage with reliable scope enforcement. Fixing this is low-risk (scope extraction only, no behavioral changes) with high leverage (all three stage paths benefit).\\n\\n### Impact\\n\\n- Blocks: Any AI agent running the work stage operates without a scope boundary document \u2014 scope creep and out-of-scope actions are not guarded.\\n- Bottleneck: `create/start.md` is a 210-line monolith; every modification to create-stage scope rules or procedure steps touches the same file, increasing merge conflict risk and making structure-level changes harder to review.\\n- Affects runtime behavior: `groom/error.md` contains a guard that checks for the presence of the scope boundary section; if the create stage is refactored without updating this guard, the guard fires as a false alarm and blocks the create workflow.\\n\\n### Benefits\\n\\n- All three workflow stages enforce scope boundaries consistently; agents load the scope document at the start of every stage.\\n- The `groom/scope.md` pattern becomes the canonical structure for all workflow stages, reducing maintenance surface.\\n- `create/start.md` shrinks from a 210-line monolith to a short checklist delegating to named files, matching the groom and work patterns.\\n- Future scope boundary changes require editing a single `scope.md` file per stage instead of locating inline content inside a combined file.\\n\\n### Expected Behavior\\n\\nWhen an AI agent runs any of the three workflow stages (create, groom, work), the first observable action is loading a `scope.md` file that defines what is in and out of scope for that stage. The stage's `start.md` file is a short checklist that delegates to named procedure files \u2014 it does not contain inline procedure content. The `groom/` directory is the reference for what this structure looks like when correct.\\n\\n### Acceptance Criteria\\n\\n1. `ls plugins/development-harness/skills/work-backlog-item/references/workflows/create/` includes `scope.md`.\\n2. `ls plugins/development-harness/skills/work-backlog-item/references/workflows/work/` includes `scope.md`.\\n3. `grep -n \\\"scope.md\\\" plugins/development-harness/skills/work-backlog-item/references/workflows/create/start.md` returns at least one match (scope.md is referenced in the checklist).\\n4. `grep -n \\\"scope.md\\\" plugins/development-harness/skills/work-backlog-item/references/workflows/work/start.md` returns at least one match.\\n5. `grep -c \\\"Create - backlog item - Scope boundary\\\" plugins/development-harness/skills/work-backlog-item/references/workflows/create/start.md` returns `0` (scope boundary section no longer inline in start.md).\\n6. `g